"<b>Why the enclosed area is the net work.</b> Internal energy depends only on the state; for an ideal gas U = n C<sub>v</sub> T. Around a closed cycle the gas returns to its starting temperature, so ΔU = {#} and the net heat in equals the net work out. Along the top of the loop the gas pushes the piston out at high pressure; along the bottom the piston pushes it back in at lower pressure. Work out minus work in is the area between the two paths. Clockwise on the PV diagram the gas does net work (an engine); counterclockwise work must be put in (a refrigerator or heat pump).",
"<b>Why no engine beats Carnot.</b> Entropy also depends only on the state, so over one cycle the gas gives off exactly the entropy it takes in. Heat Q<sub>in</sub> brings in entropy of at least Q<sub>in</sub>/T<sub>max</sub>, since the gas is never hotter than T<sub>max</sub>. Giving that entropy off at temperatures no lower than T<sub>min</sub> takes heat Q<sub>out</sub> ≥ Q<sub>in</sub> T<sub>min</sub>/T<sub>max</sub>. So W = Q<sub>in</sub> − Q<sub>out</sub> ≤ Q<sub>in</sub>({#} − T<sub>min</sub>/T<sub>max</sub>). The Carnot cycle takes in all its heat at T<sub>max</sub> and gives it all off at T<sub>min</sub>, so it reaches the limit; any other path takes in some heat below T<sub>max</sub> or gives some off above T<sub>min</sub>. On the T–S diagram the Carnot cycle is the rectangle that fills the band between the two temperatures.",
"<b>Approximations.</b> Ideal gas with constant heat capacities. Every process is quasistatic, slow enough that the gas stays uniform and follows the drawn path. The Otto, Diesel and Brayton cycles are air standard models: the same gas goes around again, burning fuel is heat added and exhaust plus fresh intake is heat removed. Real air has γ falling toward about {#} at combustion temperatures, and real engines lose to friction and fast, irreversible steps, so real efficiencies are well below these. Where the temperature changes while heat flows (isobaric and isochoric legs), a reversible version would need a series of reservoirs matched to the gas; the drawing shows one hot side and one cold side. The Stirling regenerator is ideal: it gives back exactly the heat it stored.",
